{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01S67hpoBsfed36sKqtc4xGQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AMnnuy4PPUbGdETtdR54yo","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment \"5\" \"4\" \"3\" \"2\" \"1\" soundness excitement"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1992,"cache_read_input_tokens":66597,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1992},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"41ec5f04-7858-4bc6-9c9c-717704f49743"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AMnnuy4PPUbGdETtdR54yo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment \"5\" \"4\" \"3\" \"2\" \"1\" soundness excitement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Incentives 2026\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"},{\"title\":\"ARR SAC Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"}]\n\nBased on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, I found information about the review form and its scoring criteria for both soundness and excitement.\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale\n\nThe overall assessment uses a scale where 5 = \"Consider for Award\" (papers that could be considered for outstanding paper awards, up to top 2.5%), 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle\" (papers needing substantial revisions completable by the next ARR cycle), 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle\" (papers needing substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle), and 1 = \"Do not resubmit\" (papers that need full revision or are not relevant to the ACL community).\n\n## Soundness Scoring\n\nThe soundness evaluation assesses whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims, and provides adequate support for them, considering factors like depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation.\n\nThe soundness scale ranges from 5 = \"Excellent\" (one of the most thorough studies of its type), 4 = \"Strong\" (provides sufficient support for claims with some extra experiments being optional), to 3 = \"Acceptable\" (provides sufficient support for main claims).\n\n## Excitement Scoring\n\nFor excitement, the evaluation considers how exciting the paper is subjectively and not necessarily based on what's popular, perceiving papers as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics.\n\nThe excitement scale includes 5 = \"Highly Exciting\" (would recommend to others and/or attend presentation) and 4 = \"Exciting\" (would mention to others and/or make effort to attend presentation).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"0040edf1-ea3e-47ee-8a1f-922ca98589c6","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:38:03.234Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform overall assessment \"5\" \"4\" \"3\" \"2\" \"1\" soundness excitement","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FYR7GRvSouTpQiSbEoXXhC","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Incentives 2026","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Changelog","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"},{"title":"ARR SAC Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"}]},"Based on the search results from the ACL Rolling Review website, I found information about the review form and its scoring criteria for both soundness and excitement.\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale\n\nThe overall assessment uses a scale where 5 = \"Consider for Award\" (papers that could be considered for outstanding paper awards, up to top 2.5%), 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle\" (papers needing substantial revisions completable by the next ARR cycle), 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle\" (papers needing substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle), and 1 = \"Do not resubmit\" (papers that need full revision or are not relevant to the ACL community).\n\n## Soundness Scoring\n\nThe soundness evaluation assesses whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims, and provides adequate support for them, considering factors like depth and breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity of evaluation.\n\nThe soundness scale ranges from 5 = \"Excellent\" (one of the most thorough studies of its type), 4 = \"Strong\" (provides sufficient support for claims with some extra experiments being optional), to 3 = \"Acceptable\" (provides sufficient support for main claims).\n\n## Excitement Scoring\n\nFor excitement, the evaluation considers how exciting the paper is subjectively and not necessarily based on what's popular, perceiving papers as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions/methods/datasets/metrics.\n\nThe excitement scale includes 5 = \"Highly Exciting\" (would recommend to others and/or attend presentation) and 4 = \"Exciting\" (would mention to others and/or make effort to attend presentation)."],"durationSeconds":15.722240915000002}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013J6xaSZ8KFDBYm2DV5Vo5C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, now I have the ARR/EMNLP review form details. Let me summarize the key scoring rubric:\n\n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 scale (with half-point increments)\n  - 5 = Award-worthy / Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n  - 4 = Strong Accept area\n  - 3.5 = Borderline Accept\n  - 3 = Ambivalent / Borderline\n  - 2.5 = Borderline Reject\n  - 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n  - 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n  - 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 scale\n  - 5 = Excellent\n  - 4 = Strong\n  - 3 = Acceptable\n  - 2 = Poor\n  - 1 = Major issues\n\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 scale\n  - 5 = Highly Exciting\n  - 4 = Exciting\n  - 3 = Mild interest\n  - 2 = Below average\n  - 1 = Not exciting\n\nEMNLP typically accepts around 22-25% of submissions. Now I need to assign two reviewers with different focuses—one to evaluate the methods and experiments, another to assess novelty and related work. I'll launch them as parallel agents, each receiving the full paper and context to produce independent reviews before we move into the roundtable discussion where they'll share findings and work toward consensus.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":971,"cache_read_input_tokens":68589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":971},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"39727036-6648-41f8-a55b-00555e238715"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013J6xaSZ8KFDBYm2DV5Vo5C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the EMNLP/ARR scoring rubric (1–5 scale with half-point increments for Overall Assessment, Soundness, and Excitement). Now dispatching R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":971,"cache_read_input_tokens":68589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":971},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"ad0b16bf-ee11-4f4f-90b1-681bf017d25d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013J6xaSZ8KFDBYm2DV5Vo5C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V2gPfNWiqXjYQgbXf5Y1hr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a blind peer review. Your focus areas: **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ACL Rolling Review)\nAcceptance rate ~22-25%. Scoring uses ARR rubric:\n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 (half-point increments). 5=Award-worthy (top 2.5%), 4=Strong, 3.5=Borderline Accept, 3=Ambivalent, 2.5=Borderline Reject, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit.\n- **Soundness**: 1-5. 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues.\n- **Excitement**: 1-5. 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Mild interest, 2=Below average, 1=Not exciting.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: FREIGE: Faithful Evidence-Grounded Document-Level Relation Extraction via Contrastive Evidence Discrimination\n\n**Core idea**: First LLM-based DocRE framework that jointly generates relation triples AND evidence sentences. Introduces CED (Contrastive Evidence Discrimination) scoring that contrasts gold evidence NLI scores against entity-sharing distractor NLI scores. CED is used offline (RSFT data selection + inference reranker) but fails as online RL reward (GRPO catastrophic forgetting). Also proposes EDCR metric.\n\n**Key results (DocRED test)**:\n- rel_F1: 42.8-43.8%, evi_F1: 78.9%, EDCR: 62.0-63.8%\n- SFT baseline: rel_F1 38.6%, evi_F1 77.5%, EDCR 69.5%\n- DREEAM (encoder): evi_F1 57.3%, but different evaluation protocol (attention-based)\n- Encoder SOTA rel_F1 ~63% — FREIGE is well below\n\n**Methods (Section 3)**:\n- SFT: Qwen3-4B + LoRA on DocRED\n- CED formula: margin = avg over cited sentences of [NLI(evidence, claim) - max NLI(distractor, claim)]\n- RSFT: generate N=8 candidates per doc → CED score → top-k=1 selection → fine-tune\n- CED reranker: at inference, pick highest CED-scored candidate among N=8\n- EDCR metric: proportion of cited non-gold sentences that share entities with the triple\n\n**Experiments (Section 4)**:\n- Table 1: Test results — RSFT-CED r2b (42.8/78.9/63.8) and s44 (43.8/78.3/62.0)\n- Table 2: Dev→test gap is consistent (~2pp)\n- Table 3: CED vs flat-NLI ablation — CED wins on all three metrics; flat-NLI gives nearly zero evi_F1 improvement (+0.1pp)\n- Table 4: CED composability — reranker alone gives best EDCR (-10.7pp), RSFT+reranker gives 58.5% EDCR\n- Table 5: GRPO fails across 3 reward types × multiple configs\n- Table 6-7: EDCR diagnostics by method and relation type\n- Table 8: Evidence baseline comparison (with protocol caveat)\n- Table 9: Model size ablation (1.7B/4B/8B) — consistent gains\n- Table 10: Re-DocRED — EDCR drops ~13pp, method still helps\n- Tables 11-12: top_k ablation and NLI model ablation\n- Appendix: GRPO grid, RSFT hyperparameters\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — these are the *imagined* experiments the paper should ideally cover):\n1. CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation — COVERED in Table 3\n2. Test set evaluation — COVERED in Table 1\n3. EDCR pipeline validation + cross-method diagnostic — COVERED in Tables 6-7\n4. DREEAM baseline reproduction — COVERED in Table 8\n5. CED-as-reranker — COVERED in Table 4\n6. Model size ablation (1.7B/8B) — COVERED in Table 9\n7. Re-DocRED extension — COVERED in Table 10\n8. top_k ablation — COVERED in Table 11\n9. GRPO forgetting analysis — COVERED in Table 5 (but figures missing — only table, no trajectory visualization)\n10. NLI model ablation — COVERED in Table 12\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on METHODS and EXPERIMENTS. Structure:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, with evidence — be specific about what's missing/problematic)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Missing Experiments / Analyses** (compare to the imagined experiment list above — what coverage gaps exist? what critical figures/visualizations are missing?)\n6. **Scores**: Overall Assessment, Soundness, Excitement (with brief justification for each)\n\nKey things to evaluate for methods:\n- Is CED well-motivated and technically sound?\n- Is the RSFT pipeline clearly described and reproducible?\n- Are there methodological gaps or unstated assumptions?\n- Is EDCR a well-defined metric?\n\nKey things to evaluate for experiments:\n- Statistical significance: are improvements significant? CED vs flat-NLI gaps are small (1.4pp evi_F1, 2.4pp EDCR)\n- Is the comparison with DREEAM/SAIS fair? The paper acknowledges protocol differences but is the framing honest?\n- Are ablations sufficient?\n- Coverage of imagined experiments\n- Missing visualizations (GRPO trajectory plots, scatter plots, etc.)\n- Are there confounds or alternative explanations not addressed?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is EMNLP — high bar for methodology and empirical rigor.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":971,"cache_read_input_tokens":68589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":971},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"ab1d44ed-249a-490c-9342-9d69fba73dad"}
