{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a13b8dd8beffd3eb8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01V2gPfNWiqXjYQgbXf5Y1hr","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":84234,"tool_uses":15,"duration_ms":163334},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"a08e99d3-34e5-425d-bf71-51fc2b9d3de8","session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LxDs8fR82ZgyYGzSgr1YXa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KxtJnG4vjF98r53Fd9rJZC","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"faith-evidence-ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T120227","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"\n# Review Report — Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\n**Paper**: FREIGE: Faithful Relation Extraction with Evidence via Contrastive Evidence Discrimination\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents FREIGE, an LLM-based document-level relation extraction system that jointly generates relation triples and supporting evidence sentences. The core technical contribution is Contrastive Evidence Discrimination (CED), which scores evidence quality by computing a margin between NLI entailment probabilities for cited evidence vs. the best entity-sharing distractor sentence. CED is used offline for RSFT data filtering and inference-time reranking; the paper documents its catastrophic failure as an online GRPO reward. A new metric, EDCR, is proposed to capture distractor citation rates that Evi-F1 misses.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated contrastive design.** The CED margin formula is simple, principled, and addresses a real problem: flat NLI scores cannot distinguish genuine evidence from entity-sharing distractors. The bias-cancellation argument (Section 3.3) is intuitive and supported by the low Pearson r=0.34 between CED margin and lexical overlap. The ablation in Table 2 (CED vs. flat-NLI RSFT) provides direct evidence that the contrastive signal matters: flat-NLI RSFT yields only -1.9pp EDCR improvement vs. CED's -5.5 to -7.7pp.\n\n- **Thorough negative result documentation.** The GRPO analysis (Table 5) is genuinely valuable. Showing reward overoptimization across three reward types, multiple learning rates, and with/without KL regularization makes a convincing case that this is not a hyperparameter tuning failure but a structural incompatibility between online RL and structured IE generation. The trajectory data (reward monotonically increasing while rel-F1 stays near zero) is a clean empirical signature.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The paper covers 10 of 10 imagined experiments: CED vs. flat-NLI ablation, test set evaluation, EDCR diagnostics, DREEAM reproduction, CED reranker, model size ablation, Re-DocRED, top-k ablation, GRPO analysis, and NLI model ablation. This is unusually thorough for a methods paper.\n\n- **EDCR as a diagnostic.** The observation that GRPO's best checkpoint achieves Evi-F1=56.7 but EDCR=85.8 (Table 3) is a compelling demonstration that Evi-F1 alone can mask catastrophic evidence failures. The Pearson r=-0.42 between Evi-F1 and EDCR supports their orthogonality.\n\n- **Method robustness.** Four RSFT configurations (r1, s44, r2a, r2b) all pass the evaluation criteria, with rel-F1 improvements ranging from +3.9 to +6.0pp. The method is not sensitive to LoRA scaling (alpha=128 vs. 32) or training epochs (1 vs. 3).\n\n- **Model size generality.** Table 6 shows consistent gains across 1.7B, 4B, and 8B models, with larger models benefiting more from CED filtering (EDCR 68.2 -> 63.5 -> 58.8).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### 3.1 Statistical significance is absent throughout\n\nNo confidence intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests are reported for any result. The CED vs. flat-NLI RSFT comparison is the paper's central ablation, yet the gaps are modest: +2.6pp Evi-F1 and -5.8pp EDCR. With only dev-set numbers from what appear to be single runs per configuration (except s44 which uses a different seed), it is impossible to determine whether these differences are statistically reliable. The paper reports multiple CED RSFT configurations (r1, s44, r2b) that vary by +/-2pp in rel-F1 across seeds, suggesting non-trivial variance. **This is a critical gap for an empirical contribution at EMNLP.**\n\n### 3.2 Test set results appear projected, not empirically obtained\n\nThe paper reports test set numbers (rel-F1=45.4, Evi-F1=80.2, EDCR=54.8) that match the \"expected_results\" in the review context almost exactly. The dev-to-test decay ratios are suspiciously clean (-1.2pp, -0.7pp). Given that DocRED test evaluation requires CodaLab submission (acknowledged in Section 4), and the flat-NLI RSFT test number (42.9) also aligns precisely with projections, I suspect these are extrapolated rather than submitted. If so, this is a serious concern — **the paper's main results table may not reflect actual evaluated numbers.** The authors should clarify whether CodaLab submissions were made.\n\n### 3.3 DREEAM/SAIS comparison is structurally unfair despite disclaimers\n\nThe paper acknowledges the protocol difference (generative vs. attention-based evidence) in Section 6 but still headlines the comparison \"Evi-F1 80.2 substantially surpasses DREEAM's 57.3\" in the abstract and conclusion. This framing is misleading. The generative approach has a natural recall advantage (79.3 vs. 55.9 recall, as stated in Section 5.4) because it can cite any number of sentences without an attention threshold. The 23pp Evi-F1 gap is largely explained by this recall asymmetry, not by evidence quality. A fairer comparison would report precision separately and discuss why DREEAM's precision (58.1) is lower than FREIGE's (81.2) — this likely reflects DREEAM's threshold being tuned for a different precision-recall trade-off. The paper should either (a) remove the \"surpasses\" claim from the abstract, or (b) provide a protocol-normalized comparison (e.g., at matched recall levels).\n\n### 3.4 The rel-F1 gap to encoder SOTA is large and underemphasized\n\nFREIGE achieves rel-F1=45.4 vs. encoder SOTA of 63.3 (DREEAM) — an 18pp gap. The paper frames this as \"the known trade-off of generative approaches,\" but recent LLM-based DocRE methods (LMRC: 60.69, DocKS-RAG: 63.59, listed in paper_framing.md) achieve near-encoder-parity. FREIGE's rel-F1 is substantially below these LLM baselines. The paper should directly compare against LMRC and DocKS-RAG on rel-F1, and explain whether joint evidence generation is the cause of the degradation or whether it reflects the smaller model (Qwen3-4B vs. LLaMA3.1-8B).\n\n### 3.5 CED formula has edge cases that are not discussed\n\nThe CED margin m = p_pos - p_neg relies on a max over the distractor set. But the paper's own MVP-1 analysis reveals that 44.4% of triples have *no* entity-sharing distractors (the distractor set is empty). In these cases, CED degenerates to flat NLI (or requires a default p_neg=0, making m=p_pos). This is nearly half the data. The paper does not discuss how this degeneracy is handled or how it affects the scoring. Additionally, 33.4% of gold evidence has p_pos < 0.5, meaning the NLI model does not find the gold evidence entailing — this raises questions about whether CED margins are meaningful in these cases.\n\n### 3.6 EDCR metric definition has a denominator issue\n\nEDCR = |entity-sharing non-gold cited| / |total cited|. When the model cites very few sentences, EDCR becomes noisy (small denominator). More importantly, a model that cites only one sentence per triple (always the gold one) would achieve EDCR=0 regardless of evidence quality. EDCR rewards conservative citation behavior and penalizes generous citation. The paper does not analyze how citation count varies across methods, but the memory.md notes that \"RSFT increases pred_count leading to more distractor citation\" — meaning RSFT's higher EDCR may partly reflect its generating longer outputs rather than worse evidence selection.\n\n### 3.7 Missing visualizations weaken the GRPO analysis\n\nThe GRPO catastrophic forgetting analysis (Claim 3) is presented only as a table (Table 5). The review context imagines three specific visualizations: (1) dual-axis plot of reward vs. rel-F1 over steps, (2) 3-panel comparison across reward types, (3) phase diagram of rel-F1 vs. evi-F1 trajectory. None of these appear in the paper. A trajectory plot would be far more impactful than a table for conveying the reward overoptimization pattern — this is a missed opportunity that weakens an otherwise strong negative result.\n\n### 3.8 RSFT pipeline details are incomplete for reproducibility\n\n- The SFT training details (number of epochs, learning rate, warmup, training duration) are not provided.\n- The RSFT candidate generation uses temperature=0.7, but the paper does not mention the Qwen3 generation_config override issue documented in memory.md (the model's built-in config silently overwrites the temperature). If this was not corrected in the actual experiments, the candidates may have been generated at T=0.6 with top_k=20, which would reduce diversity and affect the RSFT pipeline's effectiveness.\n- The 12.8% selection rate (2,700 from 21,000) with top_k=1 means each document contributes at most 1 training example. How are documents where all 8 candidates score below a threshold handled? Is there a minimum CED threshold, or is top-1 always selected regardless of score?\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Were the test set numbers in Table 1 obtained from actual CodaLab submissions?** If so, please provide submission IDs. If projected, please state this explicitly.\n\n2. **How is CED computed when the distractor set is empty (44.4% of triples)?** Is p_neg set to 0, or is the triple excluded from scoring?\n\n3. **Can you provide standard deviations across seeds for the CED vs. flat-NLI comparison?** The s44 vs. r1 gap (rel-F1 46.6 vs. 44.5) is larger than the CED vs. flat-NLI gap (44.5 vs. 43.8), raising concerns about whether CED's advantage is within noise.\n\n4. **Was the Qwen3 generation_config temperature override (T=0.6, top_k=20) corrected before RSFT candidate generation?** The project documentation suggests this was discovered and fixed mid-experiment.\n\n5. **Why are LMRC (rel-F1 60.69) and DocKS-RAG (rel-F1 63.59) omitted from Table 1?** These are recent LLM-based DocRE methods that would contextualize FREIGE's extraction performance.\n\n6. **What is the average number of sentences cited per triple across methods?** This is needed to interpret EDCR properly — does RSFT cite more sentences (explaining higher EDCR) or fewer?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments / Analyses\n\nComparing against the 10 imagined experiments:\n\n| # | Experiment | Status | Gap |\n|---|-----------|--------|-----|\n| 1 | CED vs flat-NLI RSFT ablation | COVERED (Table 2) | No significance tests |\n| 2 | Test set evaluation | COVERED (Table 1) | Possibly projected, not actual |\n| 3 | EDCR diagnostic table | COVERED (Table 3) | Adequate |\n| 4 | DREEAM reproduction | COVERED (Table 4) | Adequate |\n| 5 | CED reranker | COVERED (Table 4b) | Adequate |\n| 6 | Model size ablation | COVERED (Table 6) | Missing SFT Evi-F1 for 1.7B/8B |\n| 7 | Re-DocRED | COVERED (Table 7) | Adequate |\n| 8 | top-k ablation | COVERED (Table 8) | Only k=1,3; k=2 missing |\n| 9 | GRPO forgetting | COVERED (Table 5) | **Missing trajectory visualizations** |\n| 10 | NLI model ablation | COVERED (Table 9) | Adequate |\n\n**Critical missing items:**\n\n- **Statistical significance testing** across all comparisons. At minimum: bootstrap CIs for the CED vs. flat-NLI gap, and paired permutation tests.\n- **GRPO trajectory visualization** — dual-axis plot of reward/rel-F1 over training steps. The table format obscures the dynamic pattern.\n- **Direct comparison with recent LLM-based DocRE methods** (LMRC, DocKS-RAG, RelPrior) on rel-F1.\n- **Citation count analysis** — average sentences cited per triple across methods, to properly interpret EDCR.\n- **Error analysis / qualitative examples** — at least 1-2 case studies showing what CED filters vs. what flat-NLI does not.\n- **CED margin distribution** — histogram showing the distribution of margins across the dataset, including the degenerate (no-distractor) cases.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Scores\n\n### Overall Assessment: 3.5 / 5 (Borderline Accept)\n\nThe paper addresses a genuine gap (no LLM-based DocRE method produces evidence), proposes a simple and well-motivated scoring function (CED), and provides an unusually comprehensive experimental evaluation covering 10 ablations/extensions. The GRPO negative result is genuinely informative. However, the lack of statistical significance testing, the possibly projected test set numbers, the structurally unfair DREEAM comparison in the abstract, and the large rel-F1 gap to recent LLM baselines collectively prevent a confident accept. If the authors can confirm real test numbers and add significance tests, this would move to a solid 4.\n\n### Soundness: 3 / 5 (Acceptable)\n\nThe CED formulation is sound in principle but has unaddressed edge cases (empty distractor set, low p_pos). The RSFT pipeline is standard and well-applied. The EDCR metric has a denominator sensitivity issue. The GRPO analysis is thorough but lacks visualization. The absence of significance tests across modest effect sizes is the primary soundness concern.\n\n### Excitement: 3.5 / 5 (Moderate-to-Exciting)\n\nThe problem is well-chosen and practically important. CED is a clean idea with a nice bias-cancellation property. The GRPO cautionary tale adds value beyond the immediate DocRE setting. However, the rel-F1 gap to encoder/LLM SOTA tempers excitement about practical deployment, and the EDCR improvements (71->63%) suggest the method only partially solves the distractor problem it identifies.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1073,"cache_read_input_tokens":83024,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1073,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01V2gPfNWiqXjYQgbXf5Y1hr","session_id":"c37caaf9-7598-4fc8-9bcc-d45cab0964b7","uuid":"c86ceae8-360f-44cb-8f99-197ae8077815"}
